        ]
      },
      {
        "finding": "Swapping Stable Diffusion v1.5 for DALL-E 3 in MAIA's toolset raises positive-image activations, e.g. ResNet-152 3.56 to 4.64 and CLIP-RN50 3.62 to 4.34 (Table A5). The authors say the agent is bounded by its tools.",
        "proposed_claim": "The quality of automated vision-neuron descriptions depends on the quality of the input-synthesis tools used.",
        "row_ids": []
      }
    ],
    "controls": "y",
    "capability_check": "y",
    "uncertainty_reported": "y",
    "code_released": "not stated",
    "peer_reviewed": "International Conference on Machine Learning",
    "problems": "The reported predictive evaluation is for vision models, not language models. Evaluated neurons are subsets sampled across layers; the paper also notes tool failures, confirmation bias, and limitations of its synthetic-neuron detector. The layer-aggregate values in Table A3 do not report uncertainty estimates.",
    "rows_left_out": "No separate rows retained for the layer-level values in Table A3; the aggregate rows were used. Other results, including the synthetic-neuron comparison and tool ablations, are not outcomes for the listed claim versions.",
    "untested": [
      {
        "version": "V1",
        "parts": "Qwen3-32B pole shares (one job, two related jobs, not yet understood)."
      },
      {
        "version": "V2",
        "parts": "Shares across language models, dense and mixture-of-experts, at scale."
      },
      {
        "version": "V3",
        "parts": "InceptionV1 cat-and-car neuron, pose as shared feature, recursive generalization versus superposition."
      },
      {
        "version": "V4",
        "parts": "Share of interpretable neurons as a function of tools or architecture. The study shows better image tools improve description predictiveness (Table A5) but reports no interpretable-neuron share."
      },
      {
        "version": "V5",
        "parts": "Neuron-based attribution graphs without a replacement model."
      },
      {
        "version": "V6",
        "parts": "Whether explanations fitting activating examples fail to predict firing. MILAN exemplar-only labels score below MAIA, but this is a method comparison. No absolute prediction-failure rate is reported."
      },
      {
        "version": "V7",
        "parts": "Simulation-based scoring of explanations on new text and the share of neurons scoring well."
      }
    ],
    "missing": [],
    "verdict_notes": "All rows are method-comparison values from Table A3: MAIA is the study's own method, the others are baselines. They are correctly linked to no version. The paper was peer reviewed (ICML). Its synthetic neurons include built-in polysemantic (A OR B) units, but these are constructed on purpose, not measured in trained models, so they do not bear on V1–V4."
  },
